 issues  particularly that uh  the international community is concerned  about one thing we didn't mention  earlier was that the fact that never  again would afghanistan be would allow  afghanistan land or soil to be used for  the training of groups of fighters who  would threaten those countries overseas  that was one of the big concerns that  obviously led to all of that led to the  intervention of 20 years ago so with  that there was that guarantee then  specifically there was the guarantee and  answer to one of the questions to the  people who are just a kilometer or two  away from here clamoring to get into the  international airport and as we were  listening to the news conference we hear  occasionally uh gunshots ring out  sometimes the old single shot sometimes  automatic gunfire as uh taliban uh  guards and fighters who have a cordon  around the terminal try to keep our  people back it is a reminder it gives  you the context of what's happening here  but there was the assurance for all of  those people that there is no need to  run that there is a guarantee that there  will not be reprisals or grudges or a  knock on the door or interrogations as  they put it the people who are there can  go home there  there will not be any recriminations it  remains to be seen whether the people  who are in fear uh this tuesday evening  that they are going to be targeted  whether they will believe that or not  but certainly that was the message that  the taliban wanted to get across with  this news conference okay rob mcbride  there reporting live for us from  cardboard rob thank you the head of the  taliban's political office in qatar's  capital doha is heading back to  afghanistan mullah abdul hani baradar  led the group's negotiations in peace  talks with the u.s and afghan  governments he departed after meeting  qatar's foreign minister earlier today  talks between the taliban and former  afghan officials will now be held  domestically instead of in doha  let's bring in harun rahimi he's the  assistant an assistant professor of law  at the american university of  afghanistan and joins us by skype from  istanbul it's good to have you with us  again  it's quite significant is it not that  mul abdullah ghani  is heading back to afghanistan  does this give any indication do you  think of  who's going to be making up the  government's  what kind of government we're going to  see especially now that the the top  people are basing themselves in the  capital of the country uh they have just  seized  so let's just be clear that he's not the  top person in the taliban movement  he's been the head of the political  office in  doha but they have a system of  leadership where the leadership council  is at the head and mulla brother has  generally been acting as the face the  political office he can compare him to  kind of administer foreign affairs in a  sense but the head of the military camp  committee and the head of the leadership  committee are two different people um in  terms of what kind of government we can  expect now given the developments we've  observed so far  i had hoped and the hope had been that  there will be an interim government that  is neutral um that hope was lost when  kenny escaped kabul and taliban said  that they had to step in to uh fail the  vacuum they could not just wait until  the interim government uh  and they could not wait for an interim  government to take over from kanye and  then a new government will be formed  under the supervision of an interim  government that is not the case now  taliban are for all internet purposes in  charge and they will be controlling the  process through which a new government  will be formed this means that we will  be having a taliban control process  through which an inclusive government  will be formed  taliban are pretty much holding all  cards there are two considerations that  may  kind of point towards a more inclusive  system  one is international intimacy it is  some