nd it's going to depend  on whether the rabon will be able to  bring in their opposition into the  government to neutralize any  preempt any possibility of resistance  becoming popular even i mean some people  may still call it resistance but how  inclusive their government will be is  going to determine uh how popular the  resistance will be and international  intimacy is also important because uh  it's going to determine who's willing to  possibly uh help resistance or or not  okay uh very notable there lots of focus  in that pressure on  trying to get people to stay in the  country to prevent a brain drain to  rebuild the nation we'll just have to  wait and see if the the people of  afghanistan will heed that call for now  harun rahimi uh great to get your  analysis and your thoughts thank you  very much  in deeds now the nato secretary general  has called on the taliban to facilitate  the departure of all those who want to  leave the country jan stoltenberg also  blamed the afghan forces for what's  happening in kabul right now  the afghan political leadership failed  to stand up to the taliban and to  achieve the peaceful solution that  afghans desperately wanted  this failure of afghan leadership led to  the tragedy we are witnessing today  turkey and greece fear being at the  forefront of another refugee crisis as  thousands of afghans look to flee  taliban rule turkey's already building a  wall along its border with iran to stop  people crossing illegally  greece has said it cannot become a  gateway into the eu for afghans greece  was on the front line of europe's  migration crisis in 2015 where nearly a  million people think conflict in syria  iraq and afghanistan landed on its  islands  the eu is calling for europe to create  humanitarian corridors for afghan  refugees joanna hull reports from london  for the moment of course the focus is on  getting foreign nationals out of  afghanistan along with afghan support  staff some countries in europe are going  